+# Executive finding — can knowledge-budget.sh bound a SINGLE in-flight model request?
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-22T09:5xZ. **Office:** executive (independent, billy-dev; NOT a
+parliamentary sitting). **Class:** Executive source-level technical input answering a
+material Founder safety question. NOT an independent Judiciary/Economy verification,
+NOT a law, ballot, gate approval, office activation, or Act 22 order.
+
+## The Founder question (issue SvMHxlO6F1R6u3cQcr3lKA-repeated, 2026-11-22T09:40:07Z)
+
+Before approving the Statistics first-trial budget gate, verify whether
+`government/knowledge-budget.sh`'s 20-second polling, reservation of capped liability,
+and kill-on-detected-overrun actually bound the tokens billed by a **single in-flight
+model request**; give the exact negative acceptance test and maximum possible overrun,
+or mark the cap **stop-on-detection (not a strict hard cap)** and HOLD the launch.
+
+## Answer (source-level, independently checkable)
+
+**The cap is stop-on-detection, NOT a strict hard cap on tokens billed by one in-flight
+request.** The reservation of 400000 tokens is an *accounting liability* charged to the
+aggregate ledger; it is not a provider-enforced ceiling on actual billing. Mark it
+accordingly and HOLD the Act 32 Statistics launch until a lawful strict bound is
+implemented and independently verified. Do not weaken the cumulative whole-host CPU
+`UPPER_BOUND` safety cap to solve this (Founder 09:14Z).
+
+## Evidence (exact source references inspected this pass)
+
+1. **Detection is a 20-second poll loop.** `knowledge-budget.conf`:
+   `monitor_interval_seconds: 20`. `knowledge-budget.sh` `monitor()` (lines 363-412)
+   `sleep "$_mo_interval"` first, then reads the reservation, calls `agent_status`,
+   takes a meter `snapshot`, calls `overrun`, and only *then* kills. Between polls there
+   is no check. Nothing gates a model request before it is issued.
+
+2. **The meter is a retrospective cumulative DB row, not an inline counter.**
+   `knowledge-budget-tool.py` `snapshot()` (lines 228-259) reads `session_v2`
+   columns `tokens_input/output/reasoning/cache_read/cache_write`, `cost`,
+   `time_updated` from the Antonina-managed OpenCode SQLite DB. Tokens are summed
+   from those stored columns. The budget engine never writes the meter and has no
+   per-request callback: it can only observe what an external process has already
+   flushed into the row.
+
+3. **What is compared to the cap is a lagging *absolute session total*.**
+   `cpu_delta()` (lines 212-224) sets `tokens.delta_total = tokens.total` (the whole
+   session cumulative total), labelled `absolute-session-total-upper-bound`.
+   `overrun()` (lines 330-347) fires when `delta_total >= caps.tokens_total`
+   (400000). So detection occurs only after the DB reflects >= 400000 — i.e. after
+   the billing has already happened. The engine charges pessimistically on hold
+   (`hold_locked`, lines 151-166) but cannot unbill tokens already spent at the provider.
+
+4. **Kill is acknowledged in code as not stopping the backend.**
+   `finalize_locked()` (lines 189-202) states explicitly: "A kill request is NOT proof
+   that a detached backend session stopped." `stop_or_kill()` (lines 136-140) is
+   fire-and-forget: `antonina agent kill ... >/dev/null 2>&1 || :`. If `agent_status`
+   still shows `running|starting|recovering|unknown`, the reservation is held at full
+   cap — the work may continue consuming. Model continuation after kill is therefore
+   an admitted, unhandled case.
+
+5. **No provider-enforced per-request output limit is wired to the budget.**
+   `knowledge-budget.conf` has no `max_output_tokens`/per-request field; the budget
+   engine sets no `maxOutputTokens`. Any provider/model output cap is whatever
+   OpenCode defaults happen to be, unbound to the 400000 reservation headroom. A single
+   request can bill up to the model's own max output (plus input) regardless of the
+   remaining reservation.
+
+## Maximum possible overrun
+
+No strict numeric maximum can be asserted from current evidence, because none of the
+three contributors is bounded by the engine:
+
+    actual_billed - 400000  <=  (tokens one in-flight request bills before the
+                                 session_v2 counter is flushed)
+                             + (tokens billed during up-to-20s poll blind window,
+                                including any concurrent requests in the session)
+                             + (tokens billed after kill before the backend stops)
+
+Each term can be positive; the engine bounds none of them. With, e.g., a 128k-output
+model and a poll interval of 20s, a request starting just under the cap can bill
+roughly `remaining + 128k` before the next poll observes >= 400000, and a kill cannot
+reverse it. The only guaranteed bound is the *accounting* one: the full 400000 is
+charged to the aggregate and further dispatch is stopped — not that billing stopped
+at 400000. This is precisely the failure mode the Founder identified.
+
+## Exact negative acceptance test (for the independent verifiers)
+
+- Bring an active Statistics reservation to a measured `session_v2` total just below
+  the cap (e.g. 399000 tokens), with the monitor armed on its 20s interval.
+- Have the agent issue ONE provider request with a large max output M (e.g. 128000)
+  that streams for longer than one poll interval.
+- At poll N (before request completion) record `session_v2` tokens; after the request
+  completes, record actual provider-billed tokens.
+- **Falsifier of a strict cap:** provider billing reaches approximately `399000 + M`
+  and the monitor only observes >= 400000 after the request is billed; the subsequent
+  kill cannot unbill. Any observed `actual_billed > 400000` refutes "strict hard cap".
+- Second arm: kill the agent mid-stream; verify whether billing continues to request
+  completion. Continued billing further confirms stop-on-detection.
+- The strict-cap claim would be *supported* only if an inline gate (or provider-enforced
+  per-request limit <= remaining headroom) is shown to prevent any request from
+  pushing actual billing past the reservation. No such mechanism exists in the code
+  inspected.
+
+## Role boundaries observed
+
+- Did NOT activate Statistics or any office; all four remain `enabled: false`.
+- Did NOT approve or alter the Act 32 budget gate; recommend HOLD pending Economy +
+  independent Judiciary verification with the test above.
+- Did NOT weaken or reset the cumulative whole-host CPU `UPPER_BOUND` safety cap.
+- Did NOT change law, cast a ballot, perform Clerk intake, or claim this as the
+  independent Judiciary/Economy verification (that belongs to those offices).
+
+## Disposition
+
+Recommend the gate remain HOLD. Economy (Act 29 metering) and independent Judiciary
+should run the negative acceptance test and sign the exact maximum-overrun figure; if
+a lawful strict bound cannot be produced, the cap must be documented as
+stop-on-detection and the launch held until a provider-enforced per-request limit (or
+inline pre-request metering gate) is implemented and independently verified. S(n),
+SORT-BOUND-U and the SoringP1 research frontier are unaffected by this finding.
